  ('NOTE_032', (select company_id from public.company_master where company_code = 'CLI_006'), NULL, '2024-10-14', 'Naomi Kessler', 'Theo Baptiste reviewed recent transaction volumes across the account and confirmed nothing unusual to report. Follow-up items from this conversation are limited to routine documentation updates. The client did not raise any pending decisions that would require escalation on our side. Theo Baptiste noted that the executive sponsor relationship remains healthy and responsive. We confirmed the next scheduled touchpoint and agreed on standard follow-up cadence. The team confirmed their point of contact structure remains unchanged since our last interaction. The conversation touched briefly on trade_finance usage, which remains consistent with prior quarters. We discussed general market conditions in the Oil & Gas sector without specific implications for the account. No new competitive activity or concerns were raised by the client during this conversation. Theo Baptiste flagged no changes to the account''s risk profile based on this conversation. Met with Exxon Mobil''s finance team for a regular relationship check-in covering current account activity. Overall tone of the conversation was professional and consistent with the account''s established cadence.', ARRAY['routine']::text[]),
  ('NOTE_033', (select company_id from public.company_master where company_code = 'CLI_007'), (select interaction_id from public.client_interactions where evidence_code = 'INT_055'), '2026-06-26', 'Naomi Kessler', 'Alphabet''s infrastructure finance team confirmed today that the data-center financing facility went to Bellcrest Financial, citing faster turnaround on term sheets as the deciding factor. This follows the antitrust ruling and investment pullback the company announced publicly, and internally we are seeing the relationship soften in parallel rather than in isolation -- revenue has declined for four consecutive quarters and the pipeline has contracted by nearly half. The one relative bright spot is the FX product line, which has held roughly flat while everything else has weakened, suggesting the erosion is concentrated in financing-related products rather than transactional banking broadly. Sentiment on this call was clearly negative; the client used the word "frustrated" twice regarding our response times. Recommend a structured escalation with a committed response-time SLA before the next financing conversation, or we risk this becoming a broader relationship exit rather than a single lost deal.', ARRAY['ext_neg_int_neg', 'flagship']::text[]),
  ('NOTE_034', (select company_id from public.company_master where company_code = 'CLI_007'), NULL, '2025-07-15', 'Grant Elias', 'Naomi Kessler flagged no changes to the account''s risk profile based on this conversation. No new competitive activity or concerns were raised by the client during this conversation. Follow-up items from this conversation are limited to routine documentation updates. The conversation touched briefly on investment_banking usage, which remains consistent with prior quarters. Met with Alphabet''s finance team for a regular relationship check-in covering current account activity. The client did not raise any pending decisions that would require escalation on our side. Naomi Kessler noted that the executive sponsor relationship remains healthy and responsive. Naomi Kessler reviewed recent transaction volumes across the account and confirmed nothing unusual to report. We discussed general market conditions in the Internet Services sector without specific implications for the account. Overall tone of the conversation was professional and consistent with the account''s established cadence. The team confirmed their point of contact structure remains unchanged since our last interaction. We confirmed the next scheduled touchpoint and agreed on standard follow-up cadence.', ARRAY['routine']::text[]),
